WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.247 --> 00:00:03.683
- You've got no hope if
you try to pretend to be

00:00:03.683 --> 00:00:06.883
Reed Hastings or Mark
Zuckerberg or Bill Gates.

00:00:06.883 --> 00:00:07.716
Life's short.

00:00:07.716 --> 00:00:09.607
I mean, you know, why or
what are we doing this

00:00:09.607 --> 00:00:11.172
where we like, won't have a difference?

00:00:11.172 --> 00:00:12.653
We think of ourself like like alchemists.

00:00:12.653 --> 00:00:14.844
We take in money and out comes joy.

00:00:14.844 --> 00:00:16.109
I would say I've always believed

00:00:16.109 --> 00:00:18.686
that a strong mission
statement's really important

00:00:18.686 --> 00:00:21.592
and I have to say we've
never succeeded at it.

00:00:21.592 --> 00:00:24.200
- He's an American entrepreneur
and philanthropist.

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:26.300
He's the co-founder and CEO of Netflix

00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:28.612
and he serves on the board
of directors of Facebook

00:00:28.612 --> 00:00:31.214
and a number of other
non-profit organizations.

00:00:31.214 --> 00:00:34.153
He has an estimated net worth
of over a billion dollars.

00:00:34.153 --> 00:00:36.172
He's Reed Hastings and here is my take

00:00:36.172 --> 00:00:37.919
on his top ten rules to success.

00:00:37.919 --> 00:00:39.235
Rule number one is my personal favorite

00:00:39.235 --> 00:00:41.234
and make sure to stick
around all the way to the end

00:00:41.234 --> 00:00:42.909
for some special bonus clips.

00:00:42.909 --> 00:00:44.034
And as always, if Reed says something

00:00:44.034 --> 00:00:45.253
that really resonates with you,

00:00:45.253 --> 00:00:46.896
please leave in the comments below

00:00:46.896 --> 00:00:48.509
and put quotes around it so other people

00:00:48.509 --> 00:00:50.329
can be inspired, as well.

00:00:50.329 --> 00:00:51.162
Enjoy!

00:00:51.162 --> 00:00:54.031
(swooshes)

00:00:54.031 --> 00:00:57.531
(upbeat orchestral music)

00:01:04.185 --> 00:01:05.168
- You have to be authentic.

00:01:05.168 --> 00:01:08.990
I mean, you've got no hope
if you try to pretend to be

00:01:08.990 --> 00:01:12.451
Reed Hastings, or Mark
Zuckerberg, or Bill Gates,

00:01:12.451 --> 00:01:16.286
or whoever you want to think
your CEOs are that you've met.

00:01:16.286 --> 00:01:18.029
You know, that is crazy.

00:01:18.029 --> 00:01:20.812
And you got to really get
comfortable with yourself,

00:01:20.812 --> 00:01:23.145
your strengths, and weaknesses.

00:01:23.145 --> 00:01:26.337
People are inspired by authenticity

00:01:26.337 --> 00:01:29.504
and I'm just surprised how many people

00:01:30.561 --> 00:01:34.483
feel like they're acting
and/or try to act.

00:01:34.483 --> 00:01:38.685
And you may be a really
quiet person and that's fine.

00:01:38.685 --> 00:01:42.750
But if you have that
fierce will, that desire,

00:01:42.750 --> 00:01:46.204
for the institution,
the company to be great,

00:01:46.204 --> 00:01:49.189
and your own role in that is secondary,

00:01:49.189 --> 00:01:52.267
and you authentically express
that in whatever ways you do,

00:01:52.267 --> 00:01:55.564
then you'll be greatly accepted, okay?

00:01:55.564 --> 00:01:58.258
And so, that's all it
takes, is to be authentic

00:01:58.258 --> 00:02:00.157
and not try to be something else

00:02:00.157 --> 00:02:02.774
and to fundamentally care at your core

00:02:02.774 --> 00:02:04.859
about what's good for the company.

00:02:04.859 --> 00:02:06.881
And if everybody knows and you act

00:02:06.881 --> 00:02:08.446
in the notion of what's good

00:02:08.446 --> 00:02:10.458
as best as your judgement can tell,

00:02:10.458 --> 00:02:12.984
what's good for the
company, they will see that

00:02:12.984 --> 00:02:15.468
and people will follow you
and they won't be cynical.

00:02:15.468 --> 00:02:18.166
It's when you hide things,
it's when you pretend.

00:02:18.166 --> 00:02:20.059
If we don't, if we're not creative enough,

00:02:20.059 --> 00:02:22.646
if we don't push it hard enough,

00:02:22.646 --> 00:02:24.112
we've got to have some failures

00:02:24.112 --> 00:02:26.733
and so, we don't really
want to have all hits.

00:02:26.733 --> 00:02:28.851
I'm clearly glad the first ones are hits.

00:02:28.851 --> 00:02:30.532
But, you know, the sign that you're

00:02:30.532 --> 00:02:33.744
right at the edge of chaos,
the edge of creativity,

00:02:33.744 --> 00:02:35.314
is that some stuff doesn't work.

00:02:35.314 --> 00:02:38.033
- And why do you want to be there?

00:02:38.033 --> 00:02:38.866
'Cause networks don't want to be there.

00:02:38.866 --> 00:02:40.199
- Life's short.

00:02:40.199 --> 00:02:42.118
I mean, you know, why or
what are we doing this

00:02:42.118 --> 00:02:43.496
where we like, won't have a difference?

00:02:43.496 --> 00:02:45.079
- Atta-boy, right?
- You know?

00:02:45.079 --> 00:02:47.055
And so it's an aid.

00:02:47.055 --> 00:02:49.235
It's wanting to push the edge,

00:02:49.235 --> 00:02:50.568
to create something that really,

00:02:50.568 --> 00:02:52.987
the people are proud
of and that they enjoy.

00:02:52.987 --> 00:02:54.195
We collect this year about eight

00:02:54.195 --> 00:02:56.293
billion dollars of customers' money.

00:02:56.293 --> 00:02:58.776
So, thank you all of you,
for giving us your money.

00:02:58.776 --> 00:03:00.251
And what we do is we say,

00:03:00.251 --> 00:03:02.617
"That money's in trust to create joy.

00:03:02.617 --> 00:03:05.611
"We have to turn that in
to the most joy possible."

00:03:05.611 --> 00:03:07.923
And so, we look and we say for every show,

00:03:07.923 --> 00:03:09.731
if a show cost a hundred million dollars,

00:03:09.731 --> 00:03:11.150
how much joy, how much viewing

00:03:11.150 --> 00:03:13.093
did it create amongst all of you?

00:03:13.093 --> 00:03:15.842
If it cost fifty million,
or two hundred million,

00:03:15.842 --> 00:03:17.157
of course, you want
different amounts of joy.

00:03:17.157 --> 00:03:18.990
(laughing)
And so, we look at as

00:03:18.990 --> 00:03:21.353
how much joy can we create of your money?

00:03:21.353 --> 00:03:24.051
And if we turn it into joy effectively,

00:03:24.051 --> 00:03:26.598
then you're happy and you tell
your friends, and we grow.

00:03:26.598 --> 00:03:27.507
And then, we have more money

00:03:27.507 --> 00:03:29.613
next year to turn into more joy.

00:03:29.613 --> 00:03:31.907
So, we think of ourself like alchemists.

00:03:31.907 --> 00:03:33.375
We take in money and out comes joy.

00:03:33.375 --> 00:03:34.333
(laughing)

00:03:34.333 --> 00:03:35.945
I would say I've always believed

00:03:35.945 --> 00:03:38.451
that a strong mission
statement's really important

00:03:38.451 --> 00:03:41.316
and I have to say we've never
succeeded at it. (laughing)

00:03:41.316 --> 00:03:43.064
It's like this odd thing.

00:03:43.064 --> 00:03:45.130
We've had them every year for 15 years

00:03:45.130 --> 00:03:46.222
and they're constantly different

00:03:46.222 --> 00:03:48.196
and now we're all so cynical.

00:03:48.196 --> 00:03:49.964
We're like, "Come on, we're
trying to make people happy."

00:03:49.964 --> 00:03:53.519
I mean, that's the fundamental
thing in consumer service.

00:03:53.519 --> 00:03:56.678
We're trying to be
better and us and Disney

00:03:56.678 --> 00:03:58.536
and Mission aren't that different.

00:03:58.536 --> 00:04:00.879
There's a bunch of different tactics at it

00:04:00.879 --> 00:04:02.619
but we're an emotional product.

00:04:02.619 --> 00:04:04.952
We cater to the things that are important,

00:04:04.952 --> 00:04:08.866
but not necessary and I
love that part about it

00:04:08.866 --> 00:04:11.748
'cause like, when you you're
selling milk or penicillin,

00:04:11.748 --> 00:04:13.777
you're selling something
that somebody needs.

00:04:13.777 --> 00:04:16.920
And we get to sell
something you want, okay,

00:04:16.920 --> 00:04:19.447
that you choose to do
because it's really cool.

00:04:19.447 --> 00:04:22.544
It's human to be entertained

00:04:22.544 --> 00:04:25.311
and to connect to people
over entertainment.

00:04:25.311 --> 00:04:27.317
And so, we celebrate that a lot

00:04:27.317 --> 00:04:28.491
and we want to change the industry.

00:04:28.491 --> 00:04:31.312
But in terms of, like a mission statement

00:04:31.312 --> 00:04:34.205
like so many companies have,

00:04:34.205 --> 00:04:37.887
Facebook talks about a more
connected and open world.

00:04:37.887 --> 00:04:40.351
And then, you see that phrase
again and again and again.

00:04:40.351 --> 00:04:41.603
And it's a powerful mantra.

00:04:41.603 --> 00:04:43.421
We don't have an equivalent.

00:04:43.421 --> 00:04:46.326
And I think we probably
would've been stronger if we do.

00:04:46.326 --> 00:04:49.715
In the early days, it was to connect

00:04:49.715 --> 00:04:52.709
people with movies they'll love.

00:04:52.709 --> 00:04:53.878
And it's okay, but it's kind of like

00:04:53.878 --> 00:04:55.423
the long, kind of awkward phrase.

00:04:55.423 --> 00:04:57.200
Connect people with movies they'll love.

00:04:57.200 --> 00:04:58.950
It's just not punchy.

00:05:00.299 --> 00:05:03.603
And then, so like, we used
it, we had t-shirts, you know.

00:05:03.603 --> 00:05:05.377
And then, it didn't
really motivate anyone,

00:05:05.377 --> 00:05:06.903
didn't really stick.

00:05:06.903 --> 00:05:09.918
But we knew we were trying to
make people happy with DVDs.

00:05:09.918 --> 00:05:12.730
And then, eventually it was
dreaming and original content.

00:05:12.730 --> 00:05:14.714
So it didn't hold us back much.

00:05:14.714 --> 00:05:15.687
- What I find interesting about you,

00:05:15.687 --> 00:05:17.294
the way you conduct your business is that

00:05:17.294 --> 00:05:19.537
when stuff doesn't work
out, you apologize.

00:05:19.537 --> 00:05:21.792
You go out and say, "I made
a mistake," and all that.

00:05:21.792 --> 00:05:23.758
What was that the first
time you had to go out there

00:05:23.758 --> 00:05:25.005
and say, "Sorry, this was wrong."

00:05:25.005 --> 00:05:27.942
And I remember the increase,
the rate increased, right?

00:05:27.942 --> 00:05:28.996
And then you were like,
"Well, forget about it."

00:05:28.996 --> 00:05:31.203
Like, what was that experience
like for you as a boss

00:05:31.203 --> 00:05:35.645
when you're like, "I
have to go and own this."

00:05:35.645 --> 00:05:38.637
- Going through marriage
counseling 20 years ago...

00:05:38.637 --> 00:05:41.676
(audience laughing)

00:05:41.676 --> 00:05:44.457
was really how I moved to become

00:05:44.457 --> 00:05:46.964
a better manager and leader.
- [Host] Really?

00:05:46.964 --> 00:05:49.818
- And yeah, you know,
this marriage counselor

00:05:49.818 --> 00:05:52.884
was able to get me to see that I was often

00:05:52.884 --> 00:05:54.724
lying to myself and to my wife.

00:05:54.724 --> 00:05:57.439
You know, saying, "Oh family
is the most important thing."

00:05:57.439 --> 00:05:59.759
And then I'd stay at work all night.

00:05:59.759 --> 00:06:02.806
And that the value of honesty is

00:06:02.806 --> 00:06:04.909
that people can take honesty,

00:06:04.909 --> 00:06:05.867
even if it's not necessarily what they

00:06:05.867 --> 00:06:08.680
want to hear, if it's really sincere.

00:06:08.680 --> 00:06:09.904
- So then the question is Reed,

00:06:09.904 --> 00:06:11.998
what do you do to not lose the culture

00:06:11.998 --> 00:06:14.887
if you believe you've got
a great one, early on?

00:06:14.887 --> 00:06:16.193
- Yeah, I mean one of
the most common questions

00:06:16.193 --> 00:06:18.810
new employee ask is, "How
do we preserve the culture?"

00:06:18.810 --> 00:06:20.869
And I say, "Well to preserve something,

00:06:20.869 --> 00:06:23.728
"to pickle it, is the wrong solution.

00:06:23.728 --> 00:06:25.263
"We're not trying to preserve the culture,

00:06:25.263 --> 00:06:26.548
"we're trying to make it better.

00:06:26.548 --> 00:06:29.276
"And it's materially better
than it was five years ago,"

00:06:29.276 --> 00:06:32.037
and I'll cite a couple
of specific examples.

00:06:32.037 --> 00:06:34.472
And then say, "Well, your job,

00:06:34.472 --> 00:06:35.875
"in addition to doing your role,

00:06:35.875 --> 00:06:38.317
"is to figure out how
do we get even better."

00:06:38.317 --> 00:06:41.372
'Cause it's only when you
struggle to get better,

00:06:41.372 --> 00:06:45.090
that you really keep it vital and alive.

00:06:45.090 --> 00:06:46.612
So, you always have to be saying,

00:06:46.612 --> 00:06:48.133
"Yeah it's pretty good culture,

00:06:48.133 --> 00:06:49.695
"but I'm sure it can be better.

00:06:49.695 --> 00:06:51.784
"How can we be better?"

00:06:51.784 --> 00:06:54.910
And so, it's that struggle to
improve that keeps it fresh.

00:06:54.910 --> 00:06:57.125
You know, we're pretty good
at movies and TV shows.

00:06:57.125 --> 00:06:59.005
And so, the competition that we

00:06:59.005 --> 00:07:01.763
worry about is the substitution.

00:07:01.763 --> 00:07:04.128
Again, when people are
spending time on Snapchat,

00:07:04.128 --> 00:07:07.415
or Facebook video, or
YouTube, it's not Netflix,

00:07:07.415 --> 00:07:09.581
or some new app that isn't yet invented.

00:07:09.581 --> 00:07:12.069
And so, in the long-term,
you have to believe

00:07:12.069 --> 00:07:14.064
that movies and TV shows will be

00:07:14.064 --> 00:07:15.930
sort of like the opera and the novel.

00:07:15.930 --> 00:07:18.673
Pretty nichey businesses
and human entertainment

00:07:18.673 --> 00:07:20.706
will have moved on to something new.

00:07:20.706 --> 00:07:23.703
So, yeah, we're gettin' really
good at movies and TV shows,

00:07:23.703 --> 00:07:26.485
we're in the beginning
of a great run on that.

00:07:26.485 --> 00:07:28.076
But in the long-term,
there'll be substitutes.

00:07:28.076 --> 00:07:30.011
There's substitutes for
everything that come up

00:07:30.011 --> 00:07:31.249
and then, the ultimate challenge

00:07:31.249 --> 00:07:32.554
for us is can we figure out that

00:07:32.554 --> 00:07:34.962
or what that new form of entertainment is?

00:07:34.962 --> 00:07:37.476
Is that VR, is it gaming, is it AR.

00:07:37.476 --> 00:07:40.482
In '03 and '04 we're growing really fast.

00:07:40.482 --> 00:07:43.069
'05, Blockbuster attacks.

00:07:43.069 --> 00:07:45.534
And so, we thought we were very clever.

00:07:45.534 --> 00:07:48.859
We came up with a number
of ways to counterattack.

00:07:48.859 --> 00:07:52.249
And remember that
everybody's got every DVD

00:07:52.249 --> 00:07:53.457
so you've got basically the same.

00:07:53.457 --> 00:07:55.798
Blockbuster and us had
the same content offering,

00:07:55.798 --> 00:07:59.293
'cause it's non-exclusive
licensing to get DVDs.

00:07:59.293 --> 00:08:01.952
So, we could differentiate on our service.

00:08:01.952 --> 00:08:06.108
Fulfillment levels were
99% and theirs were 89.

00:08:06.108 --> 00:08:08.615
But that's like a really abstract
for a customer, you know,

00:08:08.615 --> 00:08:12.081
thinking of signing up
doesn't mean anything, right?

00:08:12.081 --> 00:08:13.513
And then, they discounted massively

00:08:13.513 --> 00:08:15.442
so it's like half our price.

00:08:15.442 --> 00:08:17.925
And so we were losing share.

00:08:17.925 --> 00:08:21.425
So, we were struggling what to do about it

00:08:22.648 --> 00:08:25.085
so we did three or four big efforts.

00:08:25.085 --> 00:08:29.136
One is in the end of '05,
we added Netflix Friends,

00:08:29.136 --> 00:08:31.232
which was our own social network.

00:08:31.232 --> 00:08:33.165
And remember in '05, Facebook's

00:08:33.165 --> 00:08:35.442
just on a few universities, okay.

00:08:35.442 --> 00:08:37.792
So our own social network
amongst our members

00:08:37.792 --> 00:08:38.875
and if you enabled each other,

00:08:38.875 --> 00:08:42.422
you could see what each other
were renting and rating.

00:08:42.422 --> 00:08:44.491
And we thought, "Wow,
the viral effects of this

00:08:44.491 --> 00:08:48.263
"would be really powerful," et cetera.

00:08:48.263 --> 00:08:49.627
We added ad sales.

00:08:49.627 --> 00:08:52.598
Back in the day, it was
like Yahoo-type banners

00:08:52.598 --> 00:08:55.084
and you could have, we
could have, we sold banners,

00:08:55.084 --> 00:08:59.002
you know, above the choosing interface.

00:08:59.002 --> 00:09:02.966
We added used DVD sales
on our site to consumers.

00:09:02.966 --> 00:09:03.877
So, you know, of course we had some

00:09:03.877 --> 00:09:06.147
excess DVDs from four years ago,

00:09:06.147 --> 00:09:08.981
so we added an operation to
sell 'em for four bucks a pop.

00:09:08.981 --> 00:09:13.148
And, it's a different logistics
but we added all that.

00:09:14.189 --> 00:09:16.378
And we added Red Envelope Entertainment,

00:09:16.378 --> 00:09:20.193
which was a group that we
brought in to buy DVDs,

00:09:20.193 --> 00:09:23.294
buy films out of Sundance
and similar festivals

00:09:23.294 --> 00:09:26.855
to then, publish them on
DVDs, so getting into content.

00:09:26.855 --> 00:09:27.795
So, we did these four efforts

00:09:27.795 --> 00:09:29.878
and each one was a dozen people,

00:09:29.878 --> 00:09:33.352
15 people and made us feel great.

00:09:33.352 --> 00:09:35.091
I mean, the employees loved 'em

00:09:35.091 --> 00:09:37.559
because you know, here
was a tangible thing

00:09:37.559 --> 00:09:41.181
that was not just 98% versus 89%.

00:09:41.181 --> 00:09:43.542
Here was a thing that they
couldn't do or weren't doing

00:09:43.542 --> 00:09:45.784
and was a differentiator, right?

00:09:45.784 --> 00:09:48.656
And wasn't management so clever?

00:09:48.656 --> 00:09:52.823
And we went through these
waves of battles in '06 and '07

00:09:54.497 --> 00:09:56.414
and in the end, we won.

00:09:57.819 --> 00:10:00.034
They ended up closing down
the their online thing

00:10:00.034 --> 00:10:03.147
and two years later,
going completely bankrupt.

00:10:03.147 --> 00:10:06.327
But we looked back and
none of those four efforts

00:10:06.327 --> 00:10:09.401
made any contribution to our victory.

00:10:09.401 --> 00:10:12.521
And one by one, we had closed
them down along the way.

00:10:12.521 --> 00:10:14.452
Every single one of those four.

00:10:14.452 --> 00:10:18.334
And so, in hindsight, we
realized, when attacked,

00:10:18.334 --> 00:10:22.339
we should retreat to do the core better

00:10:22.339 --> 00:10:26.506
and not try to broaden the
surface of attack, essentially.

00:10:29.153 --> 00:10:33.481
And it was a great lesson for us of focus.

00:10:33.481 --> 00:10:36.201
And so now when people say,

00:10:36.201 --> 00:10:37.654
"Aren't you getting into news or sports?"

00:10:37.654 --> 00:10:39.925
We're like, (claps) "Absolutely not!"

00:10:39.925 --> 00:10:42.539
You know, and we're really
confident of our answer.

00:10:42.539 --> 00:10:43.952
Like, movies and TV shows on

00:10:43.952 --> 00:10:46.576
a global basis, enormous market.

00:10:46.576 --> 00:10:49.296
And so, we're much less subject to

00:10:49.296 --> 00:10:52.753
being prone to go off and
chase the shiny object

00:10:52.753 --> 00:10:56.498
to try to have something on
a checklist to differentiate.

00:10:56.498 --> 00:10:57.331
And to trust, and in hindsight if we had

00:10:57.331 --> 00:11:01.164
just gone from like, 98% perfect to 99.9,

00:11:02.361 --> 00:11:05.604
we would've done a lot
more for the business.

00:11:05.604 --> 00:11:06.721
And you know, and it's hard work.

00:11:06.721 --> 00:11:10.718
It's operational, logistics,
how to get DVDs not to break.

00:11:10.718 --> 00:11:13.598
I mean, the amount of polycarbonate
analysis we had to do.

00:11:13.598 --> 00:11:15.865
It's all this stuff.

00:11:15.865 --> 00:11:18.824
We would've beaten Blockbuster
sooner than we did.

00:11:18.824 --> 00:11:20.133
- But let's talk about values.

00:11:20.133 --> 00:11:21.208
High performance, freedom

00:11:21.208 --> 00:11:23.539
and responsibility, context not control.

00:11:23.539 --> 00:11:25.629
These are obviously not mouthed.

00:11:25.629 --> 00:11:28.121
It seems like that if you
want to do well at Netflix,

00:11:28.121 --> 00:11:29.893
you have to subscribe to the values

00:11:29.893 --> 00:11:32.015
and if you don't, you're out.

00:11:32.015 --> 00:11:34.477
- Yeah, and the reason that
we publish our culture deck

00:11:34.477 --> 00:11:35.806
is so that people can figure out

00:11:35.806 --> 00:11:37.036
before they come to Netflix,

00:11:37.036 --> 00:11:38.659
is this the right fit for them?

00:11:38.659 --> 00:11:40.332
- But what's intriguing for me,

00:11:40.332 --> 00:11:41.689
'cause you're a competitive guy is,

00:11:41.689 --> 00:11:43.650
I looked at everything that you sent out

00:11:43.650 --> 00:11:45.537
in the last five years
and I would like to think

00:11:45.537 --> 00:11:46.370
that you've told me enough to

00:11:46.370 --> 00:11:48.677
replicate Netflix, but no one can.

00:11:48.677 --> 00:11:49.944
Why is that?

00:11:49.944 --> 00:11:51.363
- You know, it's probably
a little like Starbucks.

00:11:51.363 --> 00:11:53.016
Doesn't seem that hard to make coffee

00:11:53.016 --> 00:11:56.828
and yet, one coffee chain
is amazing around the world.

00:11:56.828 --> 00:11:59.030
So, there's a lot of heart
and passion that goes in it

00:11:59.030 --> 00:12:00.720
that doesn't come across in the words.

00:12:00.720 --> 00:12:03.172
It's the execution, it's
the people at Netflix.

00:12:03.172 --> 00:12:05.950
You know, China's a very special place

00:12:05.950 --> 00:12:09.306
and if you look at the big tech companies,

00:12:09.306 --> 00:12:12.876
Apple's done very well,
Microsoft hasn't lost too much,

00:12:12.876 --> 00:12:15.290
and Google's, you know, lost a lot.

00:12:15.290 --> 00:12:16.523
So, there's quite a bit of variation

00:12:16.523 --> 00:12:18.207
despite all three of those being

00:12:18.207 --> 00:12:21.161
talented long-termed oriented companies.

00:12:21.161 --> 00:12:23.878
And when you look at Apple,
one of the amazing things is

00:12:23.878 --> 00:12:27.914
when they did the iPhone,
they negotiated and talked

00:12:27.914 --> 00:12:30.038
and built relationships for six years

00:12:30.038 --> 00:12:32.654
before the iPhone was
allowed to come to China.

00:12:32.654 --> 00:12:34.404
So from 2005 to 2011.

00:12:35.242 --> 00:12:37.049
And that took a lot of patience.

00:12:37.049 --> 00:12:39.532
And now, that's one their largest markets

00:12:39.532 --> 00:12:41.375
in the world for the iPhone.

00:12:41.375 --> 00:12:43.024
And so, what we take away from that

00:12:43.024 --> 00:12:44.558
is a sense of great patience.

00:12:44.558 --> 00:12:47.306
So, it may be soon that
we have a license in China

00:12:47.306 --> 00:12:49.099
or it may take a couple years,

00:12:49.099 --> 00:12:50.404
but we're going to be very patient.

00:12:50.404 --> 00:12:53.060
And we're looking forward to a time,

00:12:53.060 --> 00:12:55.204
a decade or two decades from now

00:12:55.204 --> 00:12:57.540
when the Chinese middle class will want

00:12:57.540 --> 00:13:00.365
and embrace the kind of
content that we have.

00:13:00.365 --> 00:13:03.144
(swooshes)

00:13:03.144 --> 00:13:04.470
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:13:04.470 --> 00:13:06.845
I made this video because
Luke asked me too.

00:13:06.845 --> 00:13:08.045
So, if there's a famous entrepreneur

00:13:08.045 --> 00:13:09.742
that you want me too profile next,

00:13:09.742 --> 00:13:11.678
leave it in the comments below
and I'll see what I can do.

00:13:11.678 --> 00:13:13.523
I'd also love to know
which of the 10 rules

00:13:13.523 --> 00:13:15.443
had the biggest impact on you and why?

00:13:15.443 --> 00:13:16.771
What change are you going
to make to your life

00:13:16.771 --> 00:13:19.236
or your business after
watching this video?

00:13:19.236 --> 00:13:20.265
Leave it in the comments

00:13:20.265 --> 00:13:21.976
and I'm going to join in the discussion.

00:13:21.976 --> 00:13:23.141
Finally, I want to give a quick shout out

00:13:23.141 --> 00:13:25.790
to one of my TDS instructors here, Felipe.

00:13:25.790 --> 00:13:26.860
Felipe, thank you so much

00:13:26.860 --> 00:13:28.268
for picking up a copy of my book.

00:13:28.268 --> 00:13:29.391
I hope you enjoy.

00:13:29.391 --> 00:13:31.976
I love the picture that
you posted on Facebook

00:13:31.976 --> 00:13:35.002
and yeah, enjoy the reading
over the holiday break, man.

00:13:35.002 --> 00:13:36.493
So thank you guys so
much again for watching.

00:13:36.493 --> 00:13:37.440
I believe in you.

00:13:37.440 --> 00:13:39.548
I hope you continue to believe in yourself

00:13:39.548 --> 00:13:41.968
and whatever your one word is.

00:13:41.968 --> 00:13:44.355
Much love, I'll see you soon.

00:13:44.355 --> 00:13:46.605
(swooshes)

00:13:50.094 --> 00:13:53.356
- Up till 2002, all we
focused on was survival.

00:13:53.356 --> 00:13:56.727
And then, once we got
public and were profitable

00:13:56.727 --> 00:13:59.232
we started thinking, "Wow,
this thing might last."

00:13:59.232 --> 00:14:03.252
And the cruelest irony would
be to not want to work there,

00:14:03.252 --> 00:14:05.020
to let become a place
we didn't want to be.

00:14:05.020 --> 00:14:06.240
And so, we tried to think through,

00:14:06.240 --> 00:14:07.241
what do we really care about?

00:14:07.241 --> 00:14:08.832
And we realized what we really care about

00:14:08.832 --> 00:14:11.379
is working with incredible
people on hard problems

00:14:11.379 --> 00:14:12.425
and that that was the essence.

00:14:12.425 --> 00:14:15.301
So we should organize everything
around supporting that,

00:14:15.301 --> 00:14:17.915
of working with just
incredibly talented people.

00:14:17.915 --> 00:14:20.693
And so, that led us to
freedom and responsibility,

00:14:20.693 --> 00:14:23.412
because incredible people
don't want to be micromanaged.

00:14:23.412 --> 00:14:25.286
And the idea is that we manage through

00:14:25.286 --> 00:14:28.566
setting context and letting people run.

00:14:28.566 --> 00:14:30.649
You got to have the right
people, you got to reinforce

00:14:30.649 --> 00:14:34.014
that with values, setting context about

00:14:34.014 --> 00:14:36.343
what the company is trying to achieve.

00:14:36.343 --> 00:14:38.064
But the idea is to build a system

00:14:38.064 --> 00:14:42.040
where amazing amounts of
service improvement happen,

00:14:42.040 --> 00:14:43.608
in the marketing, in the content,

00:14:43.608 --> 00:14:46.327
in the technology, in the user interface,

00:14:46.327 --> 00:14:48.302
without direct intervention of me.

00:14:48.302 --> 00:14:50.863
And so, some companies operate by

00:14:50.863 --> 00:14:52.556
the product genius at the top

00:14:52.556 --> 00:14:54.248
and we all know these companies.

00:14:54.248 --> 00:14:57.158
And this whole motif of, to be a great CEO

00:14:57.158 --> 00:14:59.012
you've got to be the great product person

00:14:59.012 --> 00:15:02.954
who really, you know, and
it's intoxicating and fun.

00:15:02.954 --> 00:15:04.291
But you're building incredible amounts

00:15:04.291 --> 00:15:06.608
of dependence on yourself and eventually,

00:15:06.608 --> 00:15:09.066
how well I know how Turkish consumers

00:15:09.066 --> 00:15:11.451
consume Netflix is not that well.

00:15:11.451 --> 00:15:12.987
And so your much stronger, obviously,

00:15:12.987 --> 00:15:16.863
building a distributed
set of great thinkers.

00:15:16.863 --> 00:15:19.252
People know that but in
the past what they've done

00:15:19.252 --> 00:15:21.470
is try to make everything a process.

00:15:21.470 --> 00:15:23.647
They said, "Okay, I don't
want it to be a genius,

00:15:23.647 --> 00:15:25.897
"so we're going to organize
a process for everything."

00:15:25.897 --> 00:15:27.471
And at least once in a while you heard,

00:15:27.471 --> 00:15:28.521
"We're going to dummyproof it.

00:15:28.521 --> 00:15:30.244
"It's going to be such a
good process that anybody,"

00:15:30.244 --> 00:15:31.703
this was the IBM thing.

00:15:31.703 --> 00:15:33.598
And then, if you dummyproof the process,

00:15:33.598 --> 00:15:37.636
you only get dummies to work
there to do the process.

00:15:37.636 --> 00:15:41.224
And so, that's why we're
so opposed to that,

00:15:41.224 --> 00:15:43.893
instead, so focused on
giving people great freedom.

00:15:43.893 --> 00:15:46.427
Realizing there'll be mistakes,

00:15:46.427 --> 00:15:48.796
but there'll be a lot of
positive achievement, too.

00:15:48.796 --> 00:15:51.579
- [Interviewer] So can you
give some examples of that?

00:15:51.579 --> 00:15:53.502
You talk about it generically
and it sounds great

00:15:53.502 --> 00:15:54.951
but what are some specific examples.

00:15:54.951 --> 00:15:58.412
- So, I take pride in making
as few decisions as possible,

00:15:58.412 --> 00:16:01.404
as opposed to as many as possible.

00:16:01.404 --> 00:16:04.246
And so, when something
significant changes in the content

00:16:04.246 --> 00:16:06.314
or The House of Cards purchase.

00:16:06.314 --> 00:16:07.213
Ted explained it.

00:16:07.213 --> 00:16:09.549
It was a huge purchase at that time.

00:16:09.549 --> 00:16:11.386
It was a half an hour conversation,

00:16:11.386 --> 00:16:12.855
you know, we went back and forth.

00:16:12.855 --> 00:16:13.907
I said, "Are you really sure?"

00:16:13.907 --> 00:16:14.740
"It's a great bet."

00:16:14.740 --> 00:16:16.322
"Yes, let's do it."

00:16:16.322 --> 00:16:17.890
It wasn't like me reading the script,

00:16:17.890 --> 00:16:20.860
and me meeting with Kevin
Spacey, and you know.

00:16:20.860 --> 00:16:24.407
Or equivalent examples on the marketing,

00:16:24.407 --> 00:16:27.510
or equivalent examples on the product

00:16:27.510 --> 00:16:28.960
and the evolution there.

00:16:28.960 --> 00:16:33.127
So, it's creating a sense of,
I want to make a difference

00:16:33.972 --> 00:16:35.390
and I can make a difference.

00:16:35.390 --> 00:16:38.421
And if that's what the
leader gets excited about,

00:16:38.421 --> 00:16:40.186
is that other people
are making a difference,

00:16:40.186 --> 00:16:41.336
and you've got the right people,

00:16:41.336 --> 00:16:42.772
you got to be super demanding.

00:16:42.772 --> 00:16:45.153
So, the one part is
freedom and responsibility,

00:16:45.153 --> 00:16:47.638
the other part is a
high-performance culture

00:16:47.638 --> 00:16:49.621
where we say adequate performance

00:16:49.621 --> 00:16:51.694
gets a generous severance package.

00:16:51.694 --> 00:16:55.073
And so, we look at, we say, we want to be

00:16:55.073 --> 00:16:56.749
like a Major League baseball team.

00:16:56.749 --> 00:17:00.187
And you know, you've only got
so many positions on the field

00:17:00.187 --> 00:17:03.453
and if everyone is not
incredible at what they do,

00:17:03.453 --> 00:17:04.846
then you're not going to win.

00:17:04.846 --> 00:17:06.281
- [Interviewer] So you
turn over a lot of people.

00:17:06.281 --> 00:17:07.502
- And so we turn over a lot of people.

00:17:07.502 --> 00:17:10.223
We feel like we give people a fair shot

00:17:10.223 --> 00:17:13.296
and you know, we hire pretty openly

00:17:13.296 --> 00:17:14.770
and they know what they're getting into,

00:17:14.770 --> 00:17:16.379
but we're looking for people who,

00:17:16.379 --> 00:17:19.035
for whom freedom and responsibility works,

00:17:19.035 --> 00:17:21.342
'cause they do incredible work.

00:17:21.342 --> 00:17:22.697
- Is part of the interviewing process,

00:17:22.697 --> 00:17:25.519
do you have essentially a culture check?

00:17:25.519 --> 00:17:28.736
- Yes, but not like a checklist.

00:17:28.736 --> 00:17:30.944
Culture's always something that we

00:17:30.944 --> 00:17:33.777
interview for, of kind of curiosity.

00:17:36.099 --> 00:17:37.590
It's easy to find people who say,

00:17:37.590 --> 00:17:38.827
"Oh, I read the Netflix culture deck.

00:17:38.827 --> 00:17:41.133
"I can love it, I really
want to be there."

00:17:41.133 --> 00:17:43.497
What's harder is what are the main things

00:17:43.497 --> 00:17:46.246
you disagree with and why?

00:17:46.246 --> 00:17:48.327
And when we get a blank stare we're like,

00:17:48.327 --> 00:17:50.867
"Okay, not really a
first principle thinker."

00:17:50.867 --> 00:17:53.828
And when they say,
"Well, I thought the way

00:17:53.828 --> 00:17:57.877
"that you didn't talk
about how to acculturate."

00:17:57.877 --> 00:18:01.490
And you know, "If I'm not
great on day three, am I out?

00:18:01.490 --> 00:18:05.323
"Or is there, do you
look at it like a athlete

00:18:06.309 --> 00:18:08.918
"where it's over some time
period to prove yourself?

00:18:08.918 --> 00:18:10.630
"And if so, what's that time period

00:18:10.630 --> 00:18:12.307
"and why haven't you clarified that?"

00:18:12.307 --> 00:18:13.824
Then we're like, "Oh yeah,
that's a good insight."

00:18:13.824 --> 00:18:15.489
First principle thinker.

00:18:15.489 --> 00:18:19.631
So, we're looking for
people who are curious,

00:18:19.631 --> 00:18:21.057
typically self-confident,

00:18:21.057 --> 00:18:25.224
and they're not, they're
questioning everything around them.

